    title: "Expanding Bus Rapid Transit Across the City",
    text: "Last month, city council approved a $340 million plan to expand the city's bus rapid transit (BRT) network by adding three new corridors over the next six years. Bus rapid transit differs from a standard bus route in several ways: buses run in dedicated lanes separated from regular traffic, traffic signals are timed to give buses priority at intersections, and passengers board at raised platforms that let them pay before boarding and enter through multiple doors, cutting down on the time buses spend stopped at each stop.\n\nThe first BRT corridor, completed four years ago along Fentworth Avenue, has already demonstrated the model's potential. Average travel times along that route dropped by nearly 40 percent compared to the old bus service, and ridership on the corridor has grown by 65 percent since it opened, well above the transit authority's original projections. Transit officials point out that building a BRT corridor costs roughly one-quarter as much per kilometre as building a light rail line, making it an attractive option for a city that wants to expand rapid transit without the decade-long construction timelines associated with rail.\n\nNot everyone is convinced the expansion is worth the disruption. Several business owners along the proposed Delancy Street corridor have raised concerns that two years of construction will drive away customers who currently rely on street parking, which will be removed to make room for the dedicated bus lane. Some residents have also questioned whether narrowing Delancy Street from four traffic lanes to two, plus the dedicated bus lanes, will simply shift congestion onto neighbouring residential streets rather than reducing it. The transit authority has responded by promising a small business support fund during construction and extensive signage rerouting traffic to nearby arterial roads.\n\nFunding for the expansion comes primarily from a federal infrastructure grant, which covers 60 percent of the cost, with the remaining amount split between the municipal operating budget and a modest increase to the regional gas tax. Construction on the Delancy Street corridor is expected to begin next spring, with the two remaining corridors following in subsequent years. If ridership trends mirror those seen on Fentworth Avenue, planners estimate the completed network could carry an additional 28,000 daily riders once all three corridors are finished.",
