    text: "Beginning this fall, curbside composting will become mandatory for all single-family homes within city limits, following a two-year pilot program that ran in three neighbourhoods. Under the new program, households will receive a small kitchen countertop bin and a larger outdoor cart, and food scraps, yard waste, and certain compostable packaging will be collected weekly alongside the existing recycling and garbage pickup, which is being reduced to biweekly collection to encourage participation in composting and recycling instead.\n\nCity officials say the change is driven largely by the limited remaining capacity at the regional landfill, which is projected to reach capacity within twelve years at current disposal rates. Organic waste, when it decomposes in a landfill without oxygen, produces methane, a greenhouse gas considerably more potent than carbon dioxide over a short time span. During the pilot program, participating neighbourhoods diverted an average of 35 percent of their household waste away from the landfill and into composting, a figure city officials hope to replicate citywide once the program is mandatory rather than optional.\n\nNot all residents welcomed the pilot enthusiastically. Some complained about odour from the outdoor bins during summer months, particularly before weekly collection, and a smaller number objected to the biweekly garbage collection, arguing it was insufficient for larger households. In response to pilot feedback, the city adjusted the final program design to offer households the option of a smaller kitchen bin with a charcoal filter lid to reduce odour, and large households with five or more residents can apply for an exemption allowing continued weekly garbage pickup.\n\nThe compost collected will be processed at a facility just outside city limits and turned into finished compost within roughly twelve weeks, which the city then sells at a reduced rate to local farms and offers free, in limited quantities, to residents for their own gardens each spring. Households that fail to participate at all, based on garbage cart weight monitoring showing no reduction in disposal, may eventually face a modest surcharge on their utility bill, though city officials say this measure would only be introduced after a lengthy public education period.",
