    title: "Building Credit History as a Newcomer",
    text: "For newcomers to Canada, one of the more confusing financial realities is that years of responsible financial behaviour in another country generally do not transfer over — most Canadian lenders rely on a credit history built entirely within Canada, meaning even someone with an excellent financial track record abroad effectively starts from zero. This can create a frustrating cycle: newcomers often need credit history to qualify for things like a car loan, a cellphone contract, or an apartment lease, yet they cannot build credit history without first being approved for some form of credit.\n\nFinancial counsellors who work with newcomers typically recommend starting with a secured credit card, which requires the applicant to deposit a sum of money, often between $500 and $2,000, that the bank holds as collateral and which typically becomes the card's credit limit. Because the risk to the bank is minimal, secured cards are usually easier to obtain than a standard credit card, and using one responsibly, meaning paying the balance in full and on time each month, is reported to the national credit bureaus in the same way a standard card would be, gradually establishing a credit history.\n\nSeveral major banks have also begun offering newcomer-specific banking packages that include a standard, unsecured credit card available immediately upon opening an account, without the usual credit check, provided the applicant can show proof of permanent residency or a valid work permit issued within a set period, often the past twelve months. These packages sometimes also waive standard account fees for the first year, an incentive banks say is intended to build long-term customer relationships with a demographic they recognize as historically underserved by traditional credit products.\n\nFinancial counsellors caution that building credit takes time regardless of which path a newcomer takes; most agree it takes a minimum of six months of consistent, on-time payments before a credit score becomes meaningful enough for major lenders to rely on it, and a full year or more before it opens access to more favourable interest rates. Missing even a single payment in this early period, counsellors warn, can set a newcomer's credit-building timeline back significantly, since payment history is typically the single most heavily weighted factor in Canadian credit scoring.",
