    text: "Letters to the editor — responding to: \"Should our city eliminate fares and make public transit free for all riders?\"\n\nEsteban R.: Yes, and the sooner the better. Fare collection is actually inefficient — the cost of fare boxes, enforcement staff, and payment processing eats up a meaningful chunk of the revenue fares bring in, in some cities nearly a quarter of it. Free transit would also remove a real barrier for lower-income residents who currently skip trips they can't afford, and cities that have tried limited free-fare programs have generally seen ridership rise substantially.\n\nWinnie C.: I appreciate the equity argument, but I think it overlooks where the money would actually come from. Fares currently cover a meaningful share of operating costs, and eliminating them entirely means either raising property taxes significantly or cutting service elsewhere, likely reducing frequency or coverage in the very neighbourhoods that rely on transit most. A better approach might be income-based fare subsidies for lower-income riders rather than eliminating fares for everyone, including those who could easily afford to pay.\n\nDeshawn P.: My worry with free transit is less about money and more about what happens on the vehicles themselves. In cities that removed fares, some transit workers have reported an increase in disruptive behaviour on buses and trains, partly because fare gates and drivers checking tickets served as an informal presence that discouraged certain problems. I'm not against the idea in principle, but any free-fare proposal needs a serious plan for rider safety and vehicle cleanliness, not just cheaper rides.\n\nAmara N.: I think people are overcomplicating this. Free transit is fundamentally a climate policy as much as a social one — every rider who switches from driving to transit reduces emissions, and price is one of the biggest factors influencing that choice. Yes, funding needs to be sorted out, but that's true of every public service; we don't refuse to fund schools or fire departments just because it's complicated. I'd rather see the city commit to the goal first and work out the financing details afterward.",
