    text: "Staff newsletter forum — responding to: \"Should the city require all eligible municipal employees to work from home at least three days per week?\"\n\nGrzegorz W.: I think a mandatory policy makes sense, mainly because of the office space savings. The city currently leases far more office space than it needs given how many desks sit empty on any given day, and a mandatory hybrid schedule would let us consolidate into a smaller footprint and redirect that lease money toward actual services residents rely on. Voluntary policies tend to produce inconsistent office attendance that makes space planning nearly impossible.\n\nFatoumata B.: I'd push back on making it mandatory rather than optional. Plenty of employees, myself included, actually work better in the office, whether because of a quieter home environment or simply personal preference, and forcing everyone into the same schedule ignores that people's circumstances differ. I'd rather see a flexible policy that lets each department, or even each employee, choose an arrangement suited to their actual work and living situation.\n\nRasheed O.: My concern is about equity between roles. Plenty of city jobs, like those in public works or the front counter at service centres, simply can't be done remotely, so a mandatory remote policy only benefits office-based staff, creating a two-tier system where some employees get a benefit others structurally cannot access. If the city moves forward with this, it needs to pair it with some equivalent benefit, like additional paid time off, for staff whose roles require them to be on-site every day.\n\nLindiwe M.: I actually think the debate over mandatory versus optional misses the real question, which is whether remote work is actually being measured properly. Right now the city has no consistent way of tracking whether productivity has changed since hybrid arrangements began, and any policy decision made without that data is really just a guess dressed up as a plan. Before mandating anything in either direction, I'd want to see the city commit to measuring outcomes for at least two full quarters.",
