    id: "R4-06",
    partNum: 4,
    part: "Part 4: Reading for Viewpoints",
    title: "Should Tipping Be Replaced With Built-In Service Charges?",
    text: "Restaurant industry roundtable — responding to: \"Should restaurants replace traditional tipping with a mandatory built-in service charge instead?\"\n\nSevgi Y.: As someone who has worked as a server for over a decade, I'd genuinely welcome this change. Tip income is unpredictable week to week, and it can vary based on factors that have nothing to do with service quality, like a slow weeknight or a difficult table. A built-in service charge, especially if it's pooled and distributed evenly among staff, would give servers, and kitchen staff who currently receive none of the tips, a more stable and fair income.\n\nCornelius B.: I own a mid-sized restaurant, and while I see the appeal, a mandatory service charge is taxed differently than a voluntary tip in many cases, and depending on how it's structured, it can actually complicate payroll significantly and, in some circumstances, reduce the amount that actually reaches staff after taxes and processing compared to a straightforward cash tip. I'd support the idea in principle but only after seeing a clear breakdown of how take-home pay for staff would actually compare under each system.\n\nPetronella S.: My issue as a customer is transparency, not the underlying idea. Right now, a service charge added to a bill is not always clearly explained, and diners are sometimes left wondering whether it replaces the tip entirely, supplements it, or goes toward something else like a health benefits fund for staff, which some restaurants use it for. Whatever system a restaurant uses, it needs to be stated plainly on the menu and receipt, because ambiguity leads to distrust and awkward moments at the table.\n\nIsaias M.: Honestly, I think this whole debate misses a bigger point — customers still tend to tip less overall when they know a service charge is already included, even if it's smaller than what they might have tipped voluntarily for excellent service. Top servers who consistently deliver outstanding service may actually end up earning less under a flat, pooled system than they would under traditional tipping, since pooling removes the direct financial reward for going above and beyond.",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is Sevgi Y.'s main reason for supporting a built-in service charge?", options: ["She believes tips are always higher than service charges", "Tip income is unpredictable and doesn't always reflect service quality, and pooling could create fairer income", "She opposes any change to the current system", "She believes kitchen staff should not be paid at all"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What is Cornelius B.'s main concern about mandatory service charges?", options: ["They would have no effect on payroll", "They may be taxed differently and could complicate payroll or reduce staff take-home pay", "They are illegal in most places", "They would increase costs for the restaurant owner only, not staff"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What does Cornelius B. want to see before fully supporting the change?", options: ["Nothing further; he already fully supports it", "A clear breakdown comparing staff take-home pay under each system", "An immediate ban on all tipping", "A survey of customer preferences only"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What is Petronella S.'s main concern as a customer?", options: ["The taste of the food", "A lack of transparency about what a service charge actually covers", "The speed of service", "The size of the restaurant"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What does Petronella S. believe restaurants should do regardless of which system they use?", options: ["Hide the service charge from the receipt", "Clearly state on the menu and receipt what the charge covers", "Eliminate all charges entirely", "Let each server decide individually"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What point does Isaias M. raise that the other writers do not directly address?", options: ["That service charges are illegal", "That customers tend to tip less when a service charge is already included, potentially reducing pay for top-performing servers", "That kitchen staff should receive higher pay than servers", "That restaurants should eliminate service entirely"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "According to Isaias M., who might end up earning less under a flat, pooled system?", options: ["New servers only", "Restaurant owners", "Top servers who consistently deliver outstanding service", "Kitchen staff exclusively"], answer: 2 },
      { q: "Which writer's perspective is grounded specifically in direct experience working as a server?", options: ["Sevgi Y.", "Cornelius B.", "Petronella S.", "Isaias M."], answer: 0 },
      { q: "Which writer focuses on the customer's experience rather than staff pay or business operations?", options: ["Sevgi Y.", "Cornelius B.", "Petronella S.", "Isaias M."], answer: 2 },
      { q: "Which two writers raise concerns about how the change could financially affect staff, even though they come from different perspectives?", options: ["Sevgi Y. and Petronella S.", "Cornelius B. and Isaias M.", "Sevgi Y. and Cornelius B.", "Petronella S. and Isaias M."], answer: 1 }
    ]
