    id: "R4-09",
    partNum: 4,
    part: "Part 4: Reading for Viewpoints",
    title: "Should Municipal Elections Use Ranked Ballots?",
    text: "Civic engagement forum — responding to: \"Should the city switch from a first-past-the-post system to ranked ballots for municipal elections?\"\n\nTheodoros P.: I strongly favour the switch. Under the current system, a candidate can win with well under half the vote if the opposition is split among several similar candidates, meaning the winner doesn't necessarily reflect what most voters actually wanted. Ranked ballots, where voters rank candidates in order of preference and lower-ranked candidates' votes are redistributed until someone gets a true majority, would produce winners with broader overall support.\n\nNwakaego C.: I understand the appeal, but ranked ballots are considerably harder to explain to voters, and cities that have switched report a noticeable rise in ballots being filled out incorrectly and disqualified, particularly among first-time voters and those less comfortable with the language the ballot is printed in. I'd want to see a serious voter education campaign, well-funded and multilingual, running for at least a full year before any switch, or we risk disenfranchising exactly the voters we should be trying hardest to include.\n\nBartosz L.: My issue isn't with the concept itself but with how election results get counted and reported. Ranked ballot counting takes considerably longer than a simple first-past-the-post tally, sometimes days rather than hours, since it may require multiple rounds of redistribution before a winner emerges. In a tightly contested race, that delay can fuel public distrust and conspiracy theories about the process, especially if the eventual winner wasn't the candidate who received the most first-choice votes.\n\nSaoirse M.: Honestly, I think the counting delay concern is overstated given modern software, and the disqualified ballot issue is a solvable problem through better ballot design and education, not a fundamental flaw with ranked voting itself. What matters most to me is that ranked ballots reduce the incentive for negative campaigning, since candidates need to appeal to their opponents' supporters as a possible second choice, which tends to produce a noticeably less hostile campaign season than we've seen under the current system.",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is Theodoros P.'s main argument for ranked ballots?", options: ["The current system always produces majority winners", "Under the current system, a split opposition vote can let a candidate win without majority support, unlike under ranked ballots", "Ranked ballots are simpler to count", "Ranked ballots eliminate the need for voting entirely"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What concern does Nwakaego C. raise about ranked ballots?", options: ["They are illegal in most cities", "They are harder to explain, leading to more disqualified ballots, especially among first-time or non-native-language voters", "They always take less time to count", "They eliminate the need for voter education"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What does Nwakaego C. want to see before any switch happens?", options: ["An immediate switch with no preparation", "A well-funded, multilingual voter education campaign running for at least a year", "Cancellation of the proposal entirely", "A reduction in the number of candidates allowed to run"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What is Bartosz L.'s main concern about ranked ballots?", options: ["He disagrees with the underlying concept entirely", "Counting takes considerably longer, which could fuel public distrust in close races", "Ranked ballots are always counted incorrectly", "Ranked ballots make campaigning too polite"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "Why does Bartosz L. believe counting delays could be especially problematic?", options: ["Delays have no effect on public perception", "In a tightly contested race, delay could fuel distrust, especially if the winner wasn't the first-choice leader", "Delays are illegal under election law", "Voters do not care how long counting takes"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "How does Saoirse M. respond to the counting delay concern raised by Bartosz L.?", options: ["She agrees it is a fundamental, unsolvable flaw", "She believes the concern is overstated given modern software", "She believes counting should be done by hand only", "She has not considered the issue"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "What does Saoirse M. see as the biggest benefit of ranked ballots?", options: ["Faster vote counting", "Reduced incentive for negative campaigning, since candidates need broader appeal", "Elimination of voter education needs", "Guaranteed reelection for incumbents"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "How does Saoirse M.'s view of the disqualified ballot issue compare to Nwakaego C.'s?", options: ["She believes it is unsolvable and a reason to reject ranked ballots", "She views it as a solvable problem through better design and education rather than a fundamental flaw", "She has not addressed this concern at all", "She agrees the switch should never happen"], answer: 1 },
      { q: "Which writer's concern is primarily about the speed and public perception of the vote-counting process?", options: ["Theodoros P.", "Nwakaego C.", "Bartosz L.", "Saoirse M."], answer: 2 },
      { q: "Which two writers focus their concerns primarily on voters' ability to correctly use or understand a ranked ballot system?", options: ["Theodoros P. and Bartosz L.", "Nwakaego C. and Saoirse M.", "Theodoros P. and Saoirse M.", "Bartosz L. and Nwakaego C."], answer: 1 }
    ]
