    text: "Housing policy forum — responding to: \"Should the city require new residential developments to include a minimum percentage of affordable units?\"\n\nKazimiera W.: Yes, without question. Without a mandatory requirement, developers have little financial incentive to include affordable units voluntarily, since market-rate units are simply more profitable, and the city's housing affordability crisis has only worsened over the past decade under a mostly voluntary approach. A mandatory percentage, even a modest one like 15 percent, would ensure new housing supply actually includes options for lower- and middle-income residents rather than serving only the top of the market.\n\nOleksandr B.: I develop mid-size residential buildings, and while I understand the goal, a strict mandate without any flexibility can actually make some projects financially unworkable, particularly smaller developments where the fixed costs of construction are spread across fewer units to begin with. Some cities offer developers a choice between including affordable units on-site or paying into a dedicated affordable housing fund instead, which I think achieves a similar outcome without making every single project impossible to finance.\n\nRosalind H.: My worry is less about developers and more about how \"affordable\" actually gets defined and enforced over time. In some cities, units labelled affordable at first sale later quietly become market-rate after a set number of years, or the income thresholds used to define \"affordable\" turn out to be higher than what people who are actually struggling can afford. Any mandate needs airtight, permanent affordability requirements with real enforcement, or the policy risks becoming a symbolic gesture rather than a genuine solution.\n\nTaddeo G.: I think this entire debate undersells how much of the affordability problem comes from the sheer overall shortage of housing supply, not just its distribution. Even a well-designed affordable unit mandate only affects new construction, which is a small fraction of the total housing stock in any given year, so on its own it won't meaningfully address a shortage this severe. I'd support the mandate, but only as one piece of a much larger strategy that also includes faster approval processes and upzoning to allow significantly more housing to be built overall.",
