    title: "Should the City Expand Free Public Wi-Fi Citywide?",
    text: "Digital access forum — responding to: \"Should the city fund a major expansion of free public Wi-Fi across all neighbourhoods, not just downtown?\"\n\nChiamaka O.: I fully support this. Internet access has effectively become a basic utility, and right now free public Wi-Fi is concentrated almost entirely downtown and near a handful of community centres, leaving many lower-income neighbourhoods with far fewer options for residents who can't afford a home internet plan. Expanding coverage citywide would help students complete homework, let job seekers apply online, and generally reduce the digital divide that currently maps closely onto income levels across the city.\n\nRadoslav P.: I don't disagree with the goal, but I think the city is underestimating the ongoing cost of maintaining a citywide network once it's built. Public Wi-Fi networks require constant maintenance, security updates, and equipment replacement, and cities that have built similar networks elsewhere often find the maintenance budget was underestimated at launch, leading to spotty or unreliable service within a few years as equipment ages faster than funding for upkeep keeps pace. Any proposal needs a realistic long-term maintenance budget, not just the upfront construction cost.\n\nWinifred K.: My concern is about redundancy. Most residents, even in lower-income neighbourhoods, already have some form of internet access through a mobile data plan, even if a home broadband plan is out of reach, so I question whether a citywide network is the most efficient use of limited city funds compared to, say, directly subsidizing home internet or mobile data plans for qualifying low-income households instead. A targeted subsidy might reach the people who need help more directly than a network anyone can technically use.\n\nJerzy N.: I think the security angle gets overlooked in this conversation. Open public Wi-Fi networks are notoriously easier to exploit for intercepting unencrypted data than a private home connection, and expanding a citywide open network without also funding public digital literacy education about safe usage could end up putting vulnerable users, including seniors and newcomers less familiar with online safety practices, at greater risk rather than genuinely helping them.",
